  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nWe find ourselves in an area of weak upper flow as broad ridging \ncovers the southern CONUS except for a weak low near the Great Basin \nregion, while the main westerlies are along the US-Canada border. A \nlee sfc trough has started to develop in response to the weak upper \nlow, returning southerly winds to our area. As a result, \ntemperatures are not dropping as quickly tonight compared to last \nnight, and many locations remain in the low to mid 40s. Short-range \nguidance has trended a bit warmer with temperatures around sunrise \nlooking to be in the upper 30s to low 40s. Isolated locations may \nget a little cooler, but overall not anticipating much of a concern \nfor frost this morning. South/southwesterly winds should strengthen \nduring the day today as the pressure gradient tightens over the \narea, helping to boost temperatures into the mid to upper 60s (about \n5 degrees warmer than yesterday). \n\nThe upper low to our southwest weakens and ejects some shortwave \nenergy out over our area early in the work week, but the only likely \nimpact we see from this is an increase in cloud cover. The warming \ntrend continues with southwest flow aloft, bringing highs to the 70s \nTuesday and Wednesday. Our warmth peaks Thursday and Friday as \nridging amplifies over the central CONUS, thanks to a trough digging \nacross the western US. By this time, highs are forecast in the 80s \nwith lows in the 50s, which is firmly above average for mid-October. \nEnsemble guidance continues to be in strong agreement on the medium-\nrange pattern, leading to high confidence in this dry and warm \nweather through this week. The main uncertainty in the forecast \nremains with the timing of the western trough and associated \nprecipitation chances late this week, at which point some \ndifferences can be seen in the system's evolution. Rain has trended \nlater compared to 24 hours ago, with low chances now coming on \nSaturday.",
